(mass, momentum, and energy) to be solved at each grid node. In FLOW-3D, upstream boundary conditions can be defined either by specifying flow depth or velocity or discharge, depending on the nature of the channel geometry and flow uniformity. For the downstream boundary, a flow depth or pressure boundary condition is typically assigned to allow the model to compute backwater effects and ensure numerical stability, especially in subcritical flow conditions.","position":{"start":{"line":179,"column":1},"end":{"line":179,"column":1}},"key":"jy6noBpDX6"}],"key":"Gibinc1QXt"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":181,"column":1},"end":{"line":181,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here, briefly given about BC for containing block mesh (Fig-14 a):","position":{"start":{"line":181,"column":1},"end":{"line":181,"column":1}},"key":"rotnfZ5kOT"}],"key":"f0m15RsZPX"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":183,"column":1},"end":{"line":183,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inflow: specify velocity components or volume flow rate or Pressure. For pressure, it is assigned as stagnation pressure with fluid elevation/height.","position":{"start":{"line":183,"column":1},"end":{"line":183,"column":1}},"key":"QQxqqLEPk4"}],"key":"GllZsXITw3"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":185,"column":1},"end":{"line":185,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outflow: specify zero gradient, pressure, or elevation.","position":{"start":{"line":185,"column":1},"end":{"line":185,"column":1}},"key":"mZEVHUE8eq"}],"key":"UVIFwBLnTq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":187,"column":1},"end":{"line":187,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wall: Generally, Ymin and Ymax are automatically set as symmetry boundaries for closed domains. If wall shear stress is to be neglected (i.e., assuming frictionless conditions), assigning symmetry is appropriate. Moreover, using symmetry boundaries helps reduce computational cost in cases with symmetrical flow conditions or in 2D simulations.","position":{"start":{"line":187,"column":1},"end":{"line":187,"column":1}},"key":"cyjurlsLQV"}],"key":"YWH4M6VLMw"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":189,"column":1},"end":{"line":189,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bottom (bed): Assign bottom Zmin as wall","position":{"start":{"line":189,"column":1},"end":{"line":189,"column":1}},"key":"W1WOf8FxbL"}],"key":"odCGuvWa2S"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":191,"column":1},"end":{"line":191,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Top surface: Assign Zmax   as stagnation pressure with fluid fraction as zero.","position":{"start":{"line":191,"column":1},"end":{"line":191,"column":1}},"key":"Khu4bDNTBx"}],"key":"FHOOiEDtX1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":193,"column":1},"end":{"line":193,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: For nested block mesh, symmetry boundary conditions are applied for all boundaries (Fig.14 b).","position":{"start":{"line":193,"column":1},"end":{"line":193,"column":1}},"key":"MK2Oc4yR9y"}],"key":"uljEYjIn04"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":195,"column":1},"end":{"line":195,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the initial conditions, the upstream water level is set up to the weir crest elevation. For the global initial condition (Fig-15 & Fig-16), the tailwater depth is specified to represent the downstream flow conditions.","position":{"start":{"line":195,"column":1},"end":{"line":195,"column":1}},"key":"pADVKeM5oh"}],"key":"iZO2fKNxpo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":199,"column":1},"end":{"line":199,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typical boundary conditions are summarized below.","position":{"start":{"line":199,"column":1},"end":{"line":199,"column":1}},"key":"tPXV2dasmA"}],"key":"KCLoQaMLO6"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":203,"column":1},"end":{"line":